\textbf{Spark — railway signalling’s “approach locking” and route-release, specifically the wrong-side failure case where a signal box cannot safely cancel a cleared route because a train may have seen the proceed aspect.}

A railway interlocking distinguishes two superficially identical facts: a route is presently unoccupied, and a route is safely releasable. Once a driver could have acted on a green signal, the route acquires an \emph{approach lock}; its later release is not decided by the current track circuits alone, but by a structured discharge history—did the train pass the signal, traverse each proving section, time out under a prescribed procedure, reverse, or trigger an emergency release? The ugly feature is that “nothing is there now” is not evidence that the past obligation has vanished.

Treat a hypothesized eventual period \(p\) of the Rule-30 centre column as a timetable claiming that, every \(p\) ticks, the same centre-signal aspect has recurred. Instead of asking whether a fixed strip can support that timetable, define a \emph{route-lock obligation} carried by a centre event: the least leftward/rightward spacetime contingent which, if altered, could make that event’s bit opposite while preserving the already-adjudicated centre timetable. Left permutativity says a local output admits a unique “left input” once its other two inputs are declared; Rule 30’s asymmetric truth table may make that declaration behave like clearing a route into unknown track. A periodic centre timetable would then repeatedly clear the same notional route.

The desired Rule-30 object is not a window constraint but a ledger of unresolved route releases. Each occurrence of a selected phase in the putative period opens an obligation at a particular diagonal boundary. One tries to follow its discharge: either it reaches the lone-seed boundary in a prescribed finite way, or it is handed to another obligation at a later phase. The crucial hoped-for phenomenon is \emph{wrong-side accumulation}: because Rule 30 distinguishes \(001,010,011,100\) from the additive cancellation pattern, two obligations that appear to merge may not actually cancel; their merger itself creates a new, tagged release condition. A period would therefore imply a finite cyclic ledger of obligations. The attack is to show that every such ledger has a least outstanding obligation whose discharge requires an older outstanding obligation strictly farther out, analogous to an interlocking that cannot release a route because every proposed proof that the train cleared it presupposes an earlier uncleared overlap.

This is deliberately different from ordinary backward-cone growth. The width is allowed to be enormous; the proposed invariant is the \emph{well-foundedness of release provenance}. If it exists, one seeks a ranking on obligations—perhaps a word in diagonal run structure, not a scalar distance—such that Rule-30 local transitions cannot close a cycle of releases from the lone seed. That could turn eventual periodicity directly into an impossible cyclic safety case, rather than trying to propagate a fixed-depth contradiction across distance \(\Theta(t)\).

\emph{Rule-90 gut check: this should have a real negative control, not a vacuous pass. In Rule 90, superposition gives genuine cancellation of paired route obligations, and its eventually-zero lone-seed centre schedule ought to admit a finite discharged ledger; if the proposed ledger still forbids that, it is wrong.}

\textbf{Open questions.}
- Can “least contingent capable of flipping a centre event” be made canonical without covertly becoming a fixed-width certificate?
- Does Rule 30 have a local non-cancellation law for paired provenance tags that Rule 90 visibly lacks?
- Can a periodic trace be converted into a finite cyclic release ledger without importing arbitrary outer-boundary data?

